G rows=4` and `SITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment`, and I took no act.\n\nI checked the file with the kit's own readers. The sitting parser accepts it under the combined binding, and `blessed_rows` returns exactly LLR-278, LLR-310, LLR-338 and TC-278, so the later act turn re-attests all four.\n\n**Why meaning:** the merge now accepts a re-attestation only when the row is ruled CLARITY, or MEANING with the bless mark. A plain MEANING ruling used to be enough on a released rung; now that re-attestation is refused, which is stricter. LLR-338 and LLR-310 also now require the act assembler and the merge to share one reader for which rows a verdict authorizes.\n\n**Why all four are tagged `[MEANING+BLESS]`:**\n- **The merge now matches the act turn.** A plain `[MEANING]` means the judge wouldn't bless the row, so it mustn't be re-anchored. Before this, a hand act re-attesting such a row could still merge.\n- **It fails closed and isn't retroactive.** Acts already on trunk aren't re-read, and the held-rung CLARITY rule is unchanged.\n- **The single-reader claim is true in the code.** `kitlib/sitting.judged_rows` admits a MEANING row only when it carries the bless mark, and both the merge's backing check and `adjudicate_act` call it.\n- **The tests are there.** All of TC-278's evidence exists, including the eight new bless-mark merge cases: plain MEANING refused and blessed MEANING merging, by backing check, by named verdict and in combined acts, plus the held-rung pair.\n\n**On the coordinator's question:** TC-368's two new act-side tests belong where they are. They test the shared reader (LLR-310) and its sharing with the act assembler (LLR-338), both of which TC-368 verifies. TC-278's own new tests cover the merge refusals, so nothing needs to move.\n\nThe untracked `003-ADJUDICATE-f01e04c.md.requested` is the coordinator's, and I didn't touch it.","ttft_ms":29019,"type":"result","duration_ms":124694,"uuid":"12175e48-d455-4eb5-856d-b26ab05b3ce4","ttft_stream_ms":24307,"tim